# Intro
This example is a non cosmological hydro-only homogeneous box with gas. A gas particle at the center of the box is initialized with non null metallicity, while the other particles are metal-free. *This example is used to test _constant istotropic_ metal diffusion with the GEAR diffusion chemistry models.*

The default level is 5 (N_particle = 2^(3*level)). It is quick. If you want to try the example with higher levels, we recommend using HPC facilities.

# Configure
To run this example with GEAR model,

```
./configure --with-chemistry=X --with-cooling=grackle_0 --with-stars=GEAR --with-star-formation=GEAR --with-feedback=GEAR --with-sink=GEAR --with-kernel=wendland-C2 --with-grackle=path/to/grackle
```

with
- X=`GEAR_10` for smoothed metallicity scheme,
- X=`GEAR-MF-DIFFUSION` for the parabolic diffusion scheme,
- X=`GEAR-MF-HYPERBOLIC-DIFFUSION_10` for the hyperbolic diffusion scheme.

and then

make -j

Note that you can also use the Gizmo MFM scheme to test diffusion by adding `--with-hydro=gizmo-mfm --with-riemann-solver=hllc` to the configuration step.


# ICs
The run.sh script calls `makeIC.py' script with default values. You can experiment by changing the ICs. Run `python3 makeIC.py --help` to get the list of parameters.

# Run
Type run.sh and let's go!

You can provide parameters to the running scripts, have a look inside them.

#Debugging notes
You can track the metal mass injected by feedback and diffused by the chemitry module by adding `CFLAGS="-DSWIFT_CHEMISTRY_DEBUG_CHECKS" ./configure [other parameters]`. This will also output the total diffused metal mass by each particles over the simulation. If you sum these values, you can track metal mass conservation. Notice that star formation removes metal.

# Caveats: interpreting this test

This test is deliberately harsh. It evolves a near-delta metal seed in the
wave-dominated regime of the Cattaneo/telegrapher equation (large `tau`), not
the smooth, well-resolved regime that diffusion normally operates in. The
exact solution there is a thin expanding shell, not a smooth blob: at
`tau=10`, the ballistic front alone carries more than 99% of the metal mass
at `t=5`, with front mass fraction `e^{-a t} (1 + a t + (a t)^2 / 2)` and
`a = 1 / (2 tau)`. No particle scheme at the resolutions used here can
represent a shell thinner than the interparticle spacing, so substantial
front smearing and apparent super-causal mass leakage ahead of the shell are
expected outcomes of this test, and they grow more pronounced as `tau`
increases.

There is also a hard theoretical ceiling on what any positivity-preserving
scheme can achieve in this regime. The exact 3D solution's front includes a
signed dipole layer, `-c t e^{-a t} delta'(r - c t)`: the true solution
itself carries a negative-density lobe just inside the propagating front.
Consistent with this, the exact second moment,
`<r^2> = 6 kappa (t - tau (1 - e^{-t/tau}))`, exceeds what any everywhere
positive, causal solution can carry in the wave regime. A scheme that
enforces positivity (as this one does) therefore cannot simultaneously match
causality and the exact second moment in this test — that trade-off is a
property of the equation, not a defect of the implementation. Judge the
scheme's spread and front behaviour against that positivity ceiling, not
against the signed exact solution alone.

`metal_profile.py`'s reference curves are exact, not approximate: the
y,z-marginal of the 3D solution is exactly the 1D Green's function, so
profiles along `x` can be compared directly to the analytic curve for 3D
runs. Because of the shell structure above, the ballistic front shows up as
two moving bumps (the seed's `x`-marginal shape at `x = +/- c t`), not as a
single smooth peak. The `--epsilon` argument must match the seed radius the
run was actually generated with, or the reference curve will be offset from
the data: `0.02` for older archived runs, `0.04` for the current default.

Seed discreteness matters at low resolution. At level 5, an `epsilon` below
the interparticle spacing (about 0.023) seeds only a single particle — a
maximally adversarial point source that also imprints the grid's anisotropy
onto the result. The current default, `epsilon=0.04`, seeds around 19
particles at level 5 and is the minimum needed for profiles that are
meaningful rather than dominated by discreteness noise.

The box saturates quickly at these speeds. Once `c_hyp * t + epsilon`
exceeds the box half-extent (about 0.37 kpc), the periodic wrap contaminates
all causality and spread diagnostics: metal that left one face reappears at
the opposite one. Restrict quantitative comparisons (front position, second
moment, causality checks) to times before this happens; for `tau <= 1` this
bound is reached quickly, so only early times are usable.

Finally, keep this test's relevance to production in perspective. Real GEAR
runs use hyperbolic diffusion for subgrid turbulent mixing, where `tau` is
chosen mainly for timestep relief and the metal field is comparatively
smooth; in that resolved-gradient regime (see the `MetalDiffusionDiscontinuity`
example) the scheme is accurate and strictly causal. The closest physical
analogue to the extreme setup tested here is discrete supernova injection,
which, depending on the injection weights, can indeed resemble a delta
function or a thin Gaussian ball of metal. Expect the behaviour documented
above — front smearing, positivity-driven spread beyond the ballistic
front — during the first few relaxation times after such an injection, at
an amplitude set by the local resolution rather than by this test's
parameters.
